### 3.3 Platforms and APIs for a continuous livestream (the menial part Avik asked for)

- **Instagram.** There is no general API to start a live broadcast. An encoder pushes to
  rtmps://live-upload.instagram.com:443/rtmp/<key>, with the stream key from Live Producer on instagram.com
  (desktop). The key is new for every broadcast, and sessions are reported limited to about one hour (one source
  says four), so a continuous stream is a chain of sessions, each with a fresh key. Meta has a restricted Live API
  for eligible accounts, which Restream and StreamYard use, and which removes the per-broadcast key. Sources:
  about.instagram.com Live Producer note; support.restream.io article 18180232; streamyard.com
  multistream-to-instagram; developers.facebook.com Instagram live_media reference.
- **Facebook.** POST /<page-or-user-id>/live_videos returns an RTMPS stream URL, to be used within 24 hours. Source:
  developers.facebook.com Live Video API.
- **YouTube.** rtmp://a.rtmp.youtube.com/live2/<key>. The key can persist, and 24/7 streams are allowed. The YouTube
  Data API (liveBroadcasts, liveStreams) creates and binds broadcasts. Not re-verified in this session.
- **Twitch.** rtmp://live.twitch.tv/app/<key>. The key is persistent. Not re-verified in this session.
- **Fanning out.** One encode reaches several targets through ffmpeg's tee muxer with onfail=ignore, which drops a
  failing target without stopping the rest (tested here to a file only), or through a relay service such as
  Restream.
- **Running it.** An always-on machine with outbound TCP 443 and 1935. Stream keys live only in that machine's
  environment.

